{"status":"ready","summary":"Hash joins with more than one equality key can now push a bloom filter on their first key into the probe-side table scan. Before this, the bloom filter only existed for single-key joins. Two .cpp files changed, no header changes.

1) In join_hashtable.cpp, JoinHashTable::InsertHashes: for multi-key joins, the bloom filter is no longer fed the combined row hash. Instead it gathers layout column 0 (condition 0's build key) from the chunk's row locations, hashes it with VectorOperations::Hash and inserts those hashes. Single-key joins are unchanged.
2) In physical_hash_join.cpp, CanUseBloomFilter now requires cmp == EQUAL and ht->conditions[0].comparison == EQUAL, replacing the conditions.size()==1 check. FinalizeFilters only offers the bloom filter for the filter whose join_condition[filter_idx] == 0. All other guards are kept: filtered build side, probe estimate larger than the build count, no cast, not a perfect hash table, in-memory only. The probe-side filter already uses conditions[0] for its key name and type and NullValuesAreEqual(0).

Measured with bench (2 bindings, 4 hot runs each): q09 went from 669.6 ms to 528.3 ms (-21.1%); a separate q09-only run gave -18.4%. The geomean over the visible templates is -21.1%.

Correctness checks, each compared against the same query written with `col+0` keys so no filter can be pushed down. All results matched:
- a 2-key INNER join of the large fact table against a filtered composite-key table;
- the same with NULLs injected into build key 1;
- a RIGHT join with NULLs injected into build key 0.

A VARCHAR-first-key check also ran, but both versions returned 0 rows, so it tells us nothing. I did not add a sqllogictest because test/ is off limits.","generality":"This helps any multi-column equi-join whose filtered build side is much smaller than the probe-side table scan, for example composite foreign keys or multi-column star-schema joins. The bloom filter drops non-matching probe rows inside the scan before they are hashed on all keys and probed. If the first key isn't selective (most probe rows have a matching first-key value on the build side), SelectivityOptionalFilter turns the filter off after a few vectors. In that case the only cost is gathering and hashing key 0 for each build row at finalize.

It does not apply when:
- the first condition is not a plain equality (NOT DISTINCT FROM or inequality);
- the probe key needs a cast;
- the hash table is perfect;
- the join is external (filter pushdown is skipped there already);
- the probe side is not a table scan that accepts pushed filters.","notes":"The filter is only ever on condition 0. When condition 0 is unselective but a later key is selective, there is no gain. A follow-up could build the filter on whichever equality key has the most selective min/max or the fewest distinct values, but then the build-side gather has to match the probe column exactly.

Testing tip: range()/CTE sources don't receive pushed-down filters, so they don't exercise the bloom filter. Use real table scans and compare against a `col+0 = col+0` version, which blocks pushdown.

Correctness depends on the build side hashing exactly layout column 0 with VectorOperations::Hash, the same function BFTableFilter uses on the probe side. Build rows with a NULL key 0 only add false positives, which is harmless."}

structured_output.generality:
This helps any multi-column equi-join whose filtered build side is much smaller than the probe-side table scan, for example composite foreign keys or multi-column star-schema joins. The bloom filter drops non-matching probe rows inside the scan before they are hashed on all keys and probed. If the first key isn't selective (most probe rows have a matching first-key value on the build side), SelectivityOptionalFilter turns the filter off after a few vectors. In that case the only cost is gathering and hashing key 0 for each build row at finalize.

It does not apply when:
- the first condition is not a plain equality (NOT DISTINCT FROM or inequality);
- the probe key needs a cast;
- the hash table is perfect;
- the join is external (filter pushdown is skipped there already);
- the probe side is not a table scan that accepts pushed filters.
